vaulthalla for Debian
---------------------

Install policy
==============

Package install is intentionally low-prompt and conservative:

- no routine debconf prompts in the default install path
- optional local PostgreSQL bootstrap is attempted only when local PostgreSQL is
  already present and healthy
- optional nginx integration is attempted only when nginx is already present and
  host checks indicate low-risk automation
- package install does not bind initial super-admin Linux UID
- the only prompt is the existing-database choice described below, and only on
  an interactive (re)install that finds Vaulthalla data it cannot authenticate to
- maintainer scripts never block indefinitely: every `systemctl`, `psql`,
  `nginx -t` and `udevadm` call is bounded by a timeout, and the FUSE mount root
  `/mnt/vaulthalla` is only inspected through `/proc/self/mountinfo`

Package metadata policy
=======================

- hard runtime deps remain in `Depends` (including `fuse3` for `fusermount3`
  and `python3` for the `vh setup`/`vh teardown` lifecycle helper)
- integration components are `Recommends`:
  - `postgresql`
  - `nginx`
  - `certbot`
  - `python3-certbot-nginx`
  - `python3-certbot-dns-cloudflare`
  - `swtpm`
  - `swtpm-tools`

This keeps `apt install vaulthalla` helpful by default while preserving lean
installs via `--no-install-recommends`.

- optional preview converters are `Suggests` (separate binary packages, each
  shipping one sandboxed helper under `/usr/lib/vaulthalla/helpers/`):
  - `vaulthalla-preview-cad`: STEP/STP models -> glTF (Open CASCADE)
  - `vaulthalla-preview-media`: video posters and transcodes (FFmpeg libraries)

The daemon never links these libraries; it runs a helper only when its package
is installed and reports the preview as unavailable otherwise. Install them with
`apt install vaulthalla-preview-cad vaulthalla-preview-media`; no restart or
configuration is needed. Limits live under `preview.derive` in config.yaml.

`nodejs` is unversioned on purpose: the web console (Next.js 16) officially
wants Node.js >= 20.9, but Ubuntu 24.04 ships 18.19. A versioned dependency would
make the package uninstallable there, so the distro Node.js is accepted as-is.
Hosts that can should install a newer Node.js (for example from NodeSource).

Configuration file
==================

The packaged default config lives at `/usr/share/vaulthalla/config/config.yaml`
(with `config_template.yaml.in` beside it). postinst copies it to
`/etc/vaulthalla/config.yaml` only when that file does not exist, and never
overwrites it afterwards. `/etc/vaulthalla/config.yaml` is not a dpkg conffile:
the daemon and `vh setup remote-db` rewrite it, so upgrades must not prompt about
it. Hosts upgraded from releases that shipped it as a conffile keep their file
unchanged (dpkg tracks it as an obsolete conffile). `apt purge` removes it.

Fresh installs bind the websocket (36969) and HTTP preview (36970) servers to
127.0.0.1; browsers reach them through nginx (`/ws`, `/preview`, `/download`,
`/upload`). An existing `/etc/vaulthalla/config.yaml` is never modified.

Install and upgrade output
==========================

Maintainer-script output is operator status, not diagnostics. Every step,
the full status summary, and the output of systemctl transitions are appended
to `/var/log/vaulthalla-package.log` (root:adm 0640, outside the daemon-owned
`/var/log/vaulthalla`; rotated to `.1` past 1 MiB; removed on purge).

- A clean upgrade prints one line (old -> new version, services restarted and
  healthy, log path), plus one `Action:` line only when something is pending
  for the operator (for example the generated admin password file still exists).
- Warnings always reach the terminal, one line each.
- A hard failure (the daemon not running after the upgrade restart, a missing
  CLI socket, no TPM backend, a failed or deferred DB bootstrap, a missing
  config) prints an ERROR line and the full summary.
- Fresh installs and reinstalls print the full summary, including the database,
  TPM, nginx and web console next steps.
- `VH_PACKAGE_VERBOSE=1` prints every step and the full summary, for example
  `sudo env VH_PACKAGE_VERBOSE=1 dpkg-reconfigure vaulthalla`.

APT upgrade service restart boundary
====================================

On package upgrades, Vaulthalla restarts only active Vaulthalla units so new
package binaries and service definitions take effect. Admin-disabled or inactive
Vaulthalla units are left untouched. Each restart is bounded; a unit that does
not finish within the bound gets SIGKILL on its own cgroup (never a process-name
kill) and the upgrade continues. A slow service never fails the upgrade.

Vaulthalla ships scoped `needrestart` policy for its own units at
`/etc/needrestart/conf.d/50-vaulthalla.conf`. Ubuntu's `needrestart` apt hook
may detect Vaulthalla services after unrelated apt transactions, but the
package policy defers automatic restarts of Vaulthalla units so the core FUSE
service is not restarted outside Vaulthalla's package-controlled boundary.

Unrelated service restarts during `apt upgrade` are controlled by host-level apt
hooks such as `needrestart`, not by the Vaulthalla package.
If `example.service` should never be auto-restarted by apt, set local
`needrestart` policy in `/etc/needrestart/conf.d/local.conf`, for example:

```
$nrconf{override_rc} = {} unless exists $nrconf{override_rc};
$nrconf{override_rc}->{qr(^example\.service$)} = 0;
```

Vaulthalla does not ship third-party restart policy for other
services on the host.

`vaulthalla.service` gives up after 10 failed starts within 10 minutes (for
example, no DB credential or no TPM) instead of restarting forever. Re-arm it
with `sudo systemctl reset-failed vaulthalla` (the `vh setup` commands do this).

TPM backend behavior
====================

During postinst, Vaulthalla selects a TPM backend deterministically:

- if `/dev/tpmrm0` or `/dev/tpm0` exists, hardware TPM is used
- if no hardware TPM exists, postinst provisions/starts managed `swtpm`
  (`vaulthalla-swtpm.service`) and configures `TSS2_TCTI` for
  `vaulthalla.service`
- if managed `swtpm` fails initial startup and a swtpm AppArmor profile exists,
  postinst applies a targeted local AppArmor override for Vaulthalla's swtpm
  state path, reloads the profile, and retries startup
- if neither hardware TPM nor `swtpm` is available (for example
  `--no-install-recommends` on a VM), configure still succeeds: the TPM backend
  is reported as deferred, `vaulthalla.service` is enabled but not started, and
  `/var/lib/vaulthalla/tpm_backend_deferred` records it. Fix with
  `sudo apt install swtpm swtpm-tools && sudo dpkg-reconfigure vaulthalla`.

Managed `swtpm` state path:

- `/var/lib/swtpm/vaulthalla` (owner `vaulthalla:vaulthalla`, mode `0700`)
  - Uses distro `swtpm` AppArmor-friendly state namespace under `/var/lib/swtpm`.

Automatic DB behavior
=====================

During postinst, Vaulthalla attempts local DB bootstrap only when all of these
conditions hold:

- `VH_SKIP_DB_BOOTSTRAP` is not enabled, now or on any earlier install
  (the opt-out persists in `/var/lib/vaulthalla/db_bootstrap_disabled`)
- `database.host` in `/etc/vaulthalla/config.yaml` is local (a remote DB
  configured with `vh setup remote-db` is never touched)
- no sealed DB credential exists yet (`/var/lib/vaulthalla/.sealed_psql.blob`);
  normal upgrades therefore never touch the role or database
- `psql` exists, the local `postgres` user exists, and local PostgreSQL is
  accepting connections

If any check fails, package install continues and reports skip reason.

When bootstrap runs, postinst writes the new role password to
`/run/vaulthalla/db_password` (atomically, mode 0600) *before* creating the
`vaulthalla` role and database, so an interrupted install resumes with the same
password on the next `dpkg --configure -a`. The service consumes the file on
startup and seals the password with the TPM.

`vaulthalla.service` is only started when a DB credential exists (sealed or
pending); otherwise the summary says why it was not started.

Existing database without a sealed credential (reinstall after purge)
=====================================================================

`apt purge` deletes `/var/lib/vaulthalla` (including the sealed DB password)
but, by default, keeps the PostgreSQL database. A later install finds a
`vaulthalla` role/database it cannot authenticate to:

- if the database holds no Vaulthalla data (an interrupted install), it is
  adopted automatically: the role password is rotated and seeded
- otherwise, interactive installs prompt:
  `[a]dopt / [o]verwrite / a[b]ort`. EOF or an unrecognized answer aborts.
  Overwrite asks for a typed confirmation phrase.
- noninteractive installs use `VH_EXISTING_DB_ACTION=adopt|overwrite|abort`
  and default to abort

Abort stops configuration with a non-zero exit and leaves PostgreSQL untouched.
Resolve it with one of:

- `sudo env VH_EXISTING_DB_ACTION=adopt dpkg --configure -a`
- `sudo env VH_EXISTING_DB_ACTION=overwrite dpkg --configure -a`

Adopt rotates the role password as the local `postgres` superuser (fed via
stdin, never argv) and hands it to the service. Users, vault metadata and
settings are kept, but vault encryption keys and stored provider API keys are
only recoverable if the original `/var/lib/vaulthalla/.sealed_*.blob` files and
the same TPM are restored. Overwrite drops the database (`WITH (FORCE)`) and
role and bootstraps fresh. Normal upgrades never abort over this; they warn.

DB teardown behavior on remove/purge
====================================

- `apt remove vaulthalla` preserves local PostgreSQL role/database resources.
- `apt purge vaulthalla` may offer an interactive destructive confirmation to
  remove local PostgreSQL resources when role/database `vaulthalla` are
  detected and local PostgreSQL is reachable.
- Default answer is preserve (`No`).
- Noninteractive purge (`DEBIAN_FRONTEND=noninteractive` or no TTY) does not
  prompt and preserves local PostgreSQL resources.

`vh` is gone after purge, so manual cleanup uses psql directly:

- `sudo -u postgres psql -c 'DROP DATABASE IF EXISTS vaulthalla WITH (FORCE);'`
- `sudo -u postgres psql -c 'DROP ROLE IF EXISTS vaulthalla;'`

While the package is installed, `sudo vh teardown db` does the same (it stops
`vaulthalla.service` first).

Purge and data-disk mount points
================================

`/var/lib/vaulthalla` is often a dedicated data disk. Purge removes its
contents without crossing into other filesystems, keeps the mount point
directory (owned `root:root`, mode `0755`) and never fails because of it. Purge
also removes `/var/log/vaulthalla`, `/var/cache/vaulthalla-web`, the runtime
`/usr/share/vaulthalla-web/.next/cache`, the certbot deploy hook
`/etc/letsencrypt/renewal-hooks/deploy/vaulthalla-nginx-reload.sh`, and
`/etc/vaulthalla/config.yaml`. Operator-provided certbot credentials in
`/etc/vaulthalla/certbot/` are kept (certbot renewal configs still reference
them) and the purge output says how to remove them. Operator-managed test
credentials in `/etc/vaulthalla/testing/` are never shipped, touched on upgrade
or removed on purge; `/etc/vaulthalla` is only removed when it is empty.

Retired CLI socket units
========================

Releases up to 1.6.6 shipped `vaulthalla-cli.socket` and `vaulthalla-cli.service`.
The socket unit held `/run/vaulthalla/cli.sock` without anything accepting on
it, so `vh` hung once the daemon had re-bound the path. `vaulthalla.service` now
owns the socket and re-creates it if it disappears. On upgrade, postinst stops
both legacy units (bounded, honouring policy-rc.d via `deb-systemd-invoke`),
purges their enablement with `deb-systemd-helper`, removes leftover `.wants`
links, reloads systemd, restarts an active `vaulthalla.service`, and reports
whether the daemon bound `/run/vaulthalla/cli.sock` again. Purge also removes
any leftover enablement.

Reinstall after remove
======================

`apt remove` stops and disables the units. Reinstalling over the remaining
configuration files (`preinst install <old-version>`) is detected, and postinst
re-enables and starts the units instead of treating it as an upgrade.

Automatic nginx behavior
========================

During postinst, Vaulthalla attempts nginx integration only when all of these
conditions hold:

- `VH_SKIP_NGINX_CONFIG` is not enabled, now or on any earlier install
  (the opt-out persists in `/var/lib/vaulthalla/nginx_config_disabled`)
- nginx is installed and active
- no non-nginx listeners are detected on `:80`/`:443`
- no custom nginx site layout is detected
- packaged template exists at `/usr/share/vaulthalla/nginx/vaulthalla`

If checks pass on a fresh install (or reinstall), postinst installs/enables:

- `/etc/nginx/sites-available/vaulthalla`
- `/etc/nginx/sites-enabled/vaulthalla`

The distro default site (`sites-enabled/default`) holds `listen 80
default_server`, which would shadow the package site. When it is the unmodified
distro symlink (its target still matches the dpkg conffile checksum), postinst
disables it and records that in `/var/lib/vaulthalla/nginx_default_site_disabled`;
`apt remove`, `apt purge` and `vh teardown nginx` restore it. A modified default
site is never touched.

nginx is reloaded only after `nginx -t` succeeds, and postinst then requests
`http://127.0.0.1/` to verify that the Vaulthalla console answers. The summary
reports the result honestly (verified, upstream still starting, or shadowed by
another site).

Upgrades never create or re-enable the site: if an operator ran
`vh teardown nginx` or removed the link, that choice is kept.

Super-admin bootstrap policy
============================

Package install does not prompt for or pre-seed super-admin Linux UID mapping.
Initial ownership claim is handled in CLI flow; operators should run:

- `vh setup assign-admin`

Advanced overrides
==================

Use these install-time environment flags for explicit opt-out (they persist):

- `VH_SKIP_DB_BOOTSTRAP=1`
- `VH_SKIP_NGINX_CONFIG=1`

Examples:

- `VH_SKIP_DB_BOOTSTRAP=1 sudo -E apt install vaulthalla`
- `VH_SKIP_NGINX_CONFIG=1 sudo -E apt install vaulthalla`

`sudo vh setup db` and `sudo vh setup nginx` opt back in and remove the markers.

Validation flows
================

1. Default install:
   - `sudo apt install vaulthalla`
2. Lean install:
   - `sudo apt install --no-install-recommends vaulthalla`
3. DB override install:
   - `VH_SKIP_DB_BOOTSTRAP=1 sudo -E apt install vaulthalla`
4. nginx override install:
   - `VH_SKIP_NGINX_CONFIG=1 sudo -E apt install vaulthalla`
5. Verify payload:
   - `test -f /usr/share/vaulthalla-web/server.js`
   - `test -f /usr/share/vaulthalla/psql/000_schema.sql`
   - `test -f /usr/share/vaulthalla/config/config.yaml`
6. Verify services:
   - `systemctl status vaulthalla.service`
   - `test -S /run/vaulthalla/cli.sock` (owned by `vaulthalla.service`)
   - `systemctl status vaulthalla-web.service`
   - `systemctl status vaulthalla-swtpm.service` (expected active only when no hardware TPM is present)

If testing local package files directly, replace package names above with:

- `./vaulthalla_<version>_<arch>.deb`

CLI integration follow-up (Phase 3)
===================================

If package-time automation skipped optional integration steps, admins can run:

- `vh setup assign-admin`
- `vh setup db`
- `vh setup db --adopt` / `vh setup db --overwrite`
- `vh setup remote-db --host <host> --user <user> --database <name> --password-file <path>`
- `vh setup nginx`
- `vh setup nginx --certbot --domain <fqdn>`
- `vh setup nginx --domain <fqdn> --s3-domain <s3-fqdn> --certbot-dns-cloudflare <path>`
- `vh teardown nginx`

These commands are explicit integration actions and are intended for controlled
operator use. They are conservative and fail with actionable errors on
conflicts/unusable local state.

For local PostgreSQL bootstrap, `vh setup db`:

- validates packaged SQL assets under `/usr/share/vaulthalla/psql`
- creates/reuses local `vaulthalla` role and database
- seeds `/run/vaulthalla/db_password` whenever it sets a role password (new
  role, `--adopt`, `--overwrite`, or an empty database left by an interrupted
  install); passwords go to psql via stdin, never argv
- refuses an existing database with Vaulthalla data but no sealed credential
  unless `--adopt` or `--overwrite` is given
- starts/restarts `vaulthalla.service` (after `reset-failed`) and only reports
  success once the service stays active and has consumed the password handoff;
  otherwise it exits non-zero and points at `journalctl`
- leaves schema/migration deployment to normal runtime startup (`SqlDeployer`)

For explicit remote PostgreSQL configuration, `vh setup remote-db`:

- updates canonical `config.yaml` database host/port/user/name settings via
  existing config helpers
- seeds runtime-consumed DB password handoff to `/run/vaulthalla/db_password`
- starts/restarts `vaulthalla.service` so runtime picks up config + password,
  and verifies it stays active
- leaves schema/migration deployment to normal runtime startup (`SqlDeployer`)

For nginx integration, `vh setup nginx` and `vh teardown nginx` only manage the
Vaulthalla site path and markers:

- `/etc/nginx/sites-available/vaulthalla`
- `/etc/nginx/sites-enabled/vaulthalla`
- `/var/lib/vaulthalla/nginx_site_managed`
- `/var/lib/vaulthalla/nginx_default_site_disabled` (unmodified distro default
  site disabled for a catch-all Vaulthalla site; restored on teardown)

They do not claim ownership over unrelated nginx site configuration.

`vh setup nginx` is the canonical final deployment path for Vaulthalla-managed
nginx configuration. It regenerates `/etc/nginx/sites-available/vaulthalla`
from canonical runtime configuration (`/etc/vaulthalla/config.yaml`) and
runtime conventions, then validates/reloads nginx conservatively.

The packaged artifact at `/usr/share/vaulthalla/nginx/vaulthalla` is only a
staged baseline; operators should run `vh setup nginx` for final deterministic
deployment after install.

For explicit certbot-backed nginx handling, `vh setup nginx --certbot --domain <fqdn>`:

- validates root-run managed nginx state and certbot prerequisites
- can prompt (default No) to install missing certbot packages via apt on
  Debian/Ubuntu in interactive CLI context
- uses renewal-safe behavior when certbot-managed certificate state already
  exists for the requested domain
- uses deterministic managed config preparation + validation for fresh issuance
  and rolls back to prior managed config when preparation/issuance fails

For a dedicated public S3 host without an inbound HTTP challenge endpoint, use
Cloudflare DNS-01 certificate handling:

```text
vh setup nginx --domain vaulthalla.dev --s3-domain s3.vaulthalla.dev --certbot-dns-cloudflare /etc/vaulthalla/certbot/cloudflare.ini
```

The Cloudflare credentials file is a Certbot dns-cloudflare credentials file:
a root-owned regular file with mode `0600` containing

```text
dns_cloudflare_api_token = <Cloudflare API token>
```

The token needs Zone > DNS > Edit on the zones of both hostnames. Certbot
reads the file again on every renewal, so keep it in place. `--s3-domain` is
refused with `--certbot` because only the DNS-01 mode issues one certificate
covering both hostnames before Vaulthalla renders their HTTPS server blocks.
The generated managed site redirects HTTP to HTTPS and serves S3 only from the
dedicated S3 hostname.

